{
  "id": "calc2.series.taylor-sqrt-at-4",
  "course": "calc2",
  "topic": "series",
  "kind": "taylor_polynomial",
  "title": "A Taylor polynomial away from 0",
  "prompt": "Find the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 for $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ centered at $a = 4$.",
  "answer_format": "A polynomial in x. Powers of (x - 4) are fine.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "Taylor polynomial"
  ],
  "data": {
    "function": "sqrt(x)",
    "center": "4",
    "degree": 2,
    "variables": {
      "x": "positive"
    }
  },
  "answer": "2 + (x - 4)/4 - (x - 4)^2/64",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Compute f(4), f′(4) and f″(4).",
    "Use powers of (x − 4), and divide the second derivative term by 2!."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "$f(4) = 2$, $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}$ so $f'(4) = \\frac14$, $f''(x) = -\\frac{1}{4x^{3/2}}$ so $f''(4) = -\\frac{1}{32}$.",
    "$p_2(x) = 2 + \\frac14(x-4) - \\frac{1}{32}\\cdot\\frac{(x-4)^2}{2} = 2 + \\frac{x-4}{4} - \\frac{(x-4)^2}{64}$."
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "openstax-calc2",
    "section": "6.3 Taylor and Maclaurin Series",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "draft"
  },
  "examples": [
    {
      "name": "centered at 4",
      "submission": {
